1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,160
One twenty-three in the morning,

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:03,680
April twenty-sixth,

3
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,170
nineteen eighty-six

4
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:07,770
A nuclear reactor in the Soviet Union explodes

5
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:11,217
Forty years later,

6
00:00:11,217 --> 00:00:13,937
the area is still an exclusion zone

7
00:00:13,937 --> 00:00:16,557
This is the story of that fateful night at

8
00:00:16,557 --> 00:00:17,057
Chernobyl,

9
00:00:17,057 --> 00:00:19,377
and the people who stepped up to stop the

10
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:20,497
disaster

11
00:00:27,183 --> 00:00:28,613
In nineteen seventy,

12
00:00:28,613 --> 00:00:31,053
in the pine forests of northern Ukraine,

13
00:00:31,053 --> 00:00:33,740
the Soviet Union built a new city called

14
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:34,860
Pripyat

15
00:00:36,673 --> 00:00:38,743
It was home to the people who worked at

16
00:00:38,743 --> 00:00:41,023
the Chernobyl nuclear power plant,

17
00:00:41,023 --> 00:00:43,423
only about three kilometers away

18
00:00:46,084 --> 00:00:48,914
Nearly fifty thousand people lived in Pripyat

19
00:00:48,914 --> 00:00:51,234
The average age was under thirty

20
00:00:51,234 --> 00:00:53,834
The city was full of children

21
00:00:55,961 --> 00:00:56,791
It had schools,

22
00:00:56,791 --> 00:00:57,831
swimming pools,

23
00:00:57,831 --> 00:00:58,591
a cinema,

24
00:00:58,591 --> 00:01:01,311
and an amusement park that was due to open

25
00:01:01,311 --> 00:01:03,071
on May first

26
00:01:05,377 --> 00:01:08,967
The plant had four reactors of the RBMK type

27
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:11,207
They were the pride of the Soviet nuclear

28
00:01:11,207 --> 00:01:12,327
industry

29
00:01:14,202 --> 00:01:16,872
People believed nuclear power was so safe that

30
00:01:16,872 --> 00:01:19,232
it could be built right next to where people

31
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:20,352
lived

32
00:01:21,947 --> 00:01:24,097
In the spring of nineteen eighty-six,

33
00:01:24,097 --> 00:01:25,657
Pripyat was peaceful

34
00:01:25,657 --> 00:01:28,097
No one knew it would be the city's last

35
00:01:28,097 --> 00:01:29,217
spring

36
00:01:36,417 --> 00:01:38,007
On April twenty-fifth,

37
00:01:38,007 --> 00:01:40,887
reactor number four was preparing for a safety

38
00:01:40,887 --> 00:01:42,007
test

39
00:01:43,647 --> 00:01:44,677
The question was:

40
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:46,117
if the power went out,

41
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:48,597
could the slowing turbine produce enough

42
00:01:48,597 --> 00:01:51,277
electricity to run the cooling water pumps for

43
00:01:51,277 --> 00:01:52,237
about a minute,

44
00:01:52,237 --> 00:01:54,637
until the backup generators started?

45
00:01:57,451 --> 00:01:59,641
But the power grid in Kyiv needed more

46
00:01:59,641 --> 00:02:00,641
electricity

47
00:02:00,641 --> 00:02:03,241
The test was delayed by almost ten hours

48
00:02:05,913 --> 00:02:07,943
It was pushed into the night shift,

49
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:10,503
with crews that were not fully prepared for the

50
00:02:10,503 --> 00:02:11,623
test

51
00:02:13,814 --> 00:02:17,204
Deputy chief engineer Anatoly Dyatlov was in

52
00:02:17,204 --> 00:02:17,584
charge

53
00:02:17,584 --> 00:02:20,684
Shift leader Akimov and young engineer

54
00:02:20,684 --> 00:02:21,364
Toptunov,

55
00:02:21,364 --> 00:02:23,244
just twenty-five years old,

56
00:02:23,244 --> 00:02:25,644
sat at the control desk

57
00:02:27,445 --> 00:02:28,835
When the power was lowered,

58
00:02:28,835 --> 00:02:31,675
the reactor almost shut down completely

59
00:02:31,675 --> 00:02:33,315
To bring the power back up,

60
00:02:33,315 --> 00:02:35,915
they pulled out almost all the control rods

61
00:02:38,995 --> 00:02:41,545
The reactor was now extremely unstable

62
00:02:41,545 --> 00:02:44,425
But no one in the control room understood how

63
00:02:44,425 --> 00:02:46,505
dangerous it really was

64
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:55,940
At one twenty-three in the morning,

65
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:57,620
on April twenty-sixth,

66
00:02:57,620 --> 00:02:59,380
the test began

67
00:03:00,955 --> 00:03:02,545
Thirty-six seconds later,

68
00:03:02,545 --> 00:03:05,625
the AZ-5 emergency button was pressed,

69
00:03:05,625 --> 00:03:08,225
to drive all the control rods into the reactor

70
00:03:11,739 --> 00:03:14,489
But the control rods had graphite tips

71
00:03:14,489 --> 00:03:16,409
As they first entered the core,

72
00:03:16,409 --> 00:03:18,249
they made the reaction surge,

73
00:03:18,249 --> 00:03:20,329
instead of stopping it

74
00:03:22,259 --> 00:03:25,790
It was a deadly design flaw in the RBMK

75
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:26,910
reactor

76
00:03:28,522 --> 00:03:30,992
The power jumped to many times its normal

77
00:03:30,992 --> 00:03:31,392
level

78
00:03:31,392 --> 00:03:32,472
Steam exploded,

79
00:03:32,472 --> 00:03:35,192
and then a second explosion tore the reactor

80
00:03:35,192 --> 00:03:36,312
apart

81
00:03:38,821 --> 00:03:41,411
The huge lid of the reactor was blown off

82
00:03:41,411 --> 00:03:43,771
Burning graphite was thrown everywhere,

83
00:03:43,771 --> 00:03:46,451
and a beam of blue light rose straight into

84
00:03:46,451 --> 00:03:48,211
the night sky

85
00:03:50,496 --> 00:03:52,246
Two workers died that night

86
00:03:52,246 --> 00:03:54,846
The body of Valery Khodemchuk was never found

87
00:03:58,873 --> 00:04:00,183
In the control room,

88
00:04:00,183 --> 00:04:02,783
many still believed the reactor was intact

89
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:13,120
Five minutes after the explosion,

90
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:15,200
the first firefighters arrived

91
00:04:18,259 --> 00:04:20,049
They were led by two young lieutenants,

92
00:04:20,049 --> 00:04:22,449
Volodymyr Pravik and Viktor Kibenok

93
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:27,730
They thought it was an ordinary fire

94
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:30,170
No one warned them about radiation,

95
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:32,770
and they had no protective gear

96
00:04:34,643 --> 00:04:36,073
On the roof of the plant,

97
00:04:36,073 --> 00:04:37,393
the tar was burning,

98
00:04:37,393 --> 00:04:40,393
and pieces of radioactive graphite lay right

99
00:04:40,393 --> 00:04:42,153
under their feet

100
00:04:44,545 --> 00:04:46,905
They fought for hours to stop the fire from

101
00:04:46,905 --> 00:04:49,505
spreading to reactor number three next door

102
00:04:51,755 --> 00:04:53,465
By around five in the morning,

103
00:04:53,465 --> 00:04:55,585
the fires on the roof were out

104
00:04:55,585 --> 00:04:57,945
But many of the men had already started

105
00:04:57,945 --> 00:04:58,625
vomiting,

106
00:04:58,625 --> 00:05:00,385
and were exhausted

107
00:05:02,945 --> 00:05:05,375
They were flown to a hospital in Moscow

108
00:05:05,375 --> 00:05:06,855
Over the following months,

109
00:05:06,855 --> 00:05:09,615
twenty-eight plant workers and firefighters

110
00:05:09,615 --> 00:05:12,135
died of acute radiation sickness,

111
00:05:12,135 --> 00:05:14,215
including Pravik and Kibenok

112
00:05:23,117 --> 00:05:25,507
On the morning of April twenty-sixth,

113
00:05:25,507 --> 00:05:28,027
life in Pripyat went on like any other

114
00:05:28,027 --> 00:05:29,147
Saturday

115
00:05:31,749 --> 00:05:33,189
Children went to school,

116
00:05:33,189 --> 00:05:34,189
there were weddings,

117
00:05:34,189 --> 00:05:35,549
and people went fishing

118
00:05:35,549 --> 00:05:37,309
According to some accounts,

119
00:05:37,309 --> 00:05:39,749
people even stood on a bridge to watch the

120
00:05:39,749 --> 00:05:41,829
glow from the plant

121
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:44,860
No warning was given

122
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:45,620
Meanwhile,

123
00:05:45,620 --> 00:05:48,220
radiation was falling all over the city

124
00:05:51,025 --> 00:05:53,695
On the afternoon of April twenty-seventh,

125
00:05:53,695 --> 00:05:56,575
about thirty-six hours after the explosion,

126
00:05:56,575 --> 00:05:58,975
the loudspeakers announced an evacuation

127
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:04,550
People were told to pack for just three days

128
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:07,390
More than a thousand buses carried nearly fifty

129
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:09,990
thousand people away in just a few hours

130
00:06:13,266 --> 00:06:15,496
They had to leave behind their homes,

131
00:06:15,496 --> 00:06:16,576
their belongings,

132
00:06:16,576 --> 00:06:18,656
and even their pets

133
00:06:20,683 --> 00:06:21,953
They never came back

134
00:06:21,953 --> 00:06:24,913
The amusement park in Pripyat never had its

135
00:06:24,913 --> 00:06:26,353
opening day

136
00:06:34,117 --> 00:06:36,467
On the morning of April twenty-eighth,

137
00:06:36,467 --> 00:06:39,587
at the Forsmark nuclear power plant in Sweden,

138
00:06:39,587 --> 00:06:42,307
more than a thousand kilometers from Chernobyl,

139
00:06:42,307 --> 00:06:44,707
radiation alarms suddenly went off

140
00:06:47,666 --> 00:06:48,336
At first,

141
00:06:48,336 --> 00:06:50,736
they thought their own plant was leaking

142
00:06:50,736 --> 00:06:53,496
But the radiation was coming from somewhere to

143
00:06:53,496 --> 00:06:54,256
the east,

144
00:06:54,256 --> 00:06:56,336
carried by the wind

145
00:06:58,390 --> 00:07:00,820
The world started asking questions

146
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:01,700
That evening,

147
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:04,900
the TASS news agency released only a very short

148
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:05,580
statement:

149
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:08,180
there had been an accident at Chernobyl

150
00:07:10,524 --> 00:07:11,514
On May first,

151
00:07:11,514 --> 00:07:14,314
the Labor Day parade in Kyiv went ahead as

152
00:07:14,314 --> 00:07:14,794
planned,

153
00:07:14,794 --> 00:07:16,874
with many children marching

154
00:07:19,134 --> 00:07:21,164
It was not until May fourteenth,

155
00:07:21,164 --> 00:07:22,804
almost three weeks later,

156
00:07:22,804 --> 00:07:25,404
that Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev spoke

157
00:07:25,404 --> 00:07:27,804
about the disaster on television

158
00:07:30,485 --> 00:07:31,485
Years later,

159
00:07:31,485 --> 00:07:34,125
Gorbachev himself said that Chernobyl was one

160
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:36,725
of the reasons the Soviet Union collapsed

161
00:07:44,717 --> 00:07:47,027
The destroyed reactor was still burning

162
00:07:47,027 --> 00:07:49,387
The graphite inside burned fiercely,

163
00:07:49,387 --> 00:07:51,787
pushing radiation into the sky

164
00:07:54,099 --> 00:07:55,649
From April twenty-seventh,

165
00:07:55,649 --> 00:07:58,249
military helicopters flew over the open

166
00:07:58,249 --> 00:07:58,849
reactor,

167
00:07:58,849 --> 00:07:59,929
dropping sand,

168
00:07:59,929 --> 00:08:00,389
clay,

169
00:08:00,389 --> 00:08:02,149
boron and lead

170
00:08:04,493 --> 00:08:06,903
The pilots had to fly right over the

171
00:08:06,903 --> 00:08:07,883
radioactive plume,

172
00:08:07,883 --> 00:08:09,643
trip after trip

173
00:08:12,221 --> 00:08:13,491
In about two weeks,

174
00:08:13,491 --> 00:08:16,131
thousands of tonnes of material were dropped

175
00:08:16,131 --> 00:08:17,891
into the reactor

176
00:08:19,683 --> 00:08:22,753
Many pilots later suffered serious radiation

177
00:08:22,753 --> 00:08:23,633
exposure

178
00:08:23,633 --> 00:08:25,753
In October nineteen eighty-six,

179
00:08:25,753 --> 00:08:28,633
a helicopter hit a crane cable near the reactor

180
00:08:28,633 --> 00:08:29,393
and crashed,

181
00:08:29,393 --> 00:08:31,993
killing all four people on board

182
00:08:34,669 --> 00:08:37,419
The graphite fire burned for about ten days

183
00:08:37,419 --> 00:08:39,819
before it slowly died down

184
00:08:42,326 --> 00:08:45,036
But an even more frightening danger was waiting

185
00:08:45,036 --> 00:08:46,796
beneath the reactor

186
00:08:54,483 --> 00:08:56,953
Beneath the reactor were pools full of water

187
00:08:56,953 --> 00:08:59,033
for the safety systems

188
00:09:00,603 --> 00:09:03,273
Scientists feared that if the molten material

189
00:09:03,273 --> 00:09:05,033
fell through and hit the water,

190
00:09:05,033 --> 00:09:07,633
it could cause another steam explosion

191
00:09:10,059 --> 00:09:12,129
Someone had to wade into the flooded,

192
00:09:12,129 --> 00:09:14,969
highly radioactive basement to open the drain

193
00:09:14,969 --> 00:09:16,089
valves

194
00:09:17,954 --> 00:09:20,597
Three engineers volunteered for the mission:

195
00:09:20,597 --> 00:09:21,904
Alexei Ananenko,

196
00:09:21,904 --> 00:09:24,304
Valeri Bespalov and Boris Baranov

197
00:09:27,250 --> 00:09:29,960
Wearing wetsuits and carrying flashlights,

198
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:31,960
they felt their way through the dark,

199
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:34,560
and found the right valves to open

200
00:09:37,171 --> 00:09:38,401
The water was drained

201
00:09:38,401 --> 00:09:41,001
The threat of a second explosion was removed

202
00:09:44,416 --> 00:09:46,926
Many stories say they gave their lives

203
00:09:46,926 --> 00:09:48,006
But in fact,

204
00:09:48,006 --> 00:09:49,406
all three survived

205
00:09:49,406 --> 00:09:51,006
In twenty nineteen,

206
00:09:51,006 --> 00:09:53,606
they were awarded the title Hero of Ukraine

207
00:10:01,883 --> 00:10:03,393
To clean up the disaster,

208
00:10:03,393 --> 00:10:06,353
the Soviet Union mobilized about six hundred

209
00:10:06,353 --> 00:10:07,393
thousand people

210
00:10:07,393 --> 00:10:09,473
They were called liquidators

211
00:10:11,674 --> 00:10:14,424
About four hundred miners dug a tunnel beneath

212
00:10:14,424 --> 00:10:15,184
the reactor,

213
00:10:15,184 --> 00:10:17,784
to prepare for cooling the foundation

214
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:22,150
On the roof of reactor three,

215
00:10:22,150 --> 00:10:25,070
pieces of radioactive graphite lay scattered

216
00:10:25,070 --> 00:10:25,910
everywhere

217
00:10:25,910 --> 00:10:27,830
Robot vehicles were sent up,

218
00:10:27,830 --> 00:10:30,430
but the radiation destroyed their electronics

219
00:10:33,208 --> 00:10:33,958
In the end,

220
00:10:33,958 --> 00:10:35,398
they had to use people

221
00:10:35,398 --> 00:10:36,158
Soldiers,

222
00:10:36,158 --> 00:10:37,798
known as bio-robots,

223
00:10:37,798 --> 00:10:40,238
could only stay on the roof for about forty

224
00:10:40,238 --> 00:10:41,998
to ninety seconds

225
00:10:44,158 --> 00:10:45,468
They shoveled the graphite,

226
00:10:45,468 --> 00:10:47,468
threw it into the reactor below,

227
00:10:47,468 --> 00:10:49,548
and ran back down

228
00:10:51,939 --> 00:10:53,729
In about two hundred days,

229
00:10:53,729 --> 00:10:56,209
a giant shell of concrete and steel,

230
00:10:56,209 --> 00:10:57,569
called the sarcophagus,

231
00:10:57,569 --> 00:10:59,969
was built around reactor four

232
00:11:02,629 --> 00:11:04,779
By November nineteen eighty-six,

233
00:11:04,779 --> 00:11:06,619
the sarcophagus was finished

234
00:11:06,619 --> 00:11:08,499
But it was built in a hurry,

235
00:11:08,499 --> 00:11:11,099
and was only meant to last about thirty years

236
00:11:19,017 --> 00:11:20,887
In August nineteen eighty-six,

237
00:11:20,887 --> 00:11:23,647
scientist Valery Legasov led the Soviet

238
00:11:23,647 --> 00:11:25,127
delegation to Vienna,

239
00:11:25,127 --> 00:11:28,047
to report to the International Atomic Energy

240
00:11:28,047 --> 00:11:29,167
Agency

241
00:11:31,244 --> 00:11:33,674
He described the disaster in detail,

242
00:11:33,674 --> 00:11:36,274
far more openly than the world expected

243
00:11:39,270 --> 00:11:40,020
At first,

244
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:42,700
most of the blame was put on the operators

245
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:43,740
But over time,

246
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:47,380
the design flaws of the RBMK reactor came to

247
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:48,500
light

248
00:11:50,248 --> 00:11:51,785
In nineteen eighty-seven,

249
00:11:51,785 --> 00:11:54,918
Dyatlov and two plant managers were sentenced

250
00:11:54,918 --> 00:11:57,318
to ten years in prison

251
00:11:59,422 --> 00:12:02,332
Legasov kept warning about nuclear safety

252
00:12:02,332 --> 00:12:03,052
problems

253
00:12:03,052 --> 00:12:05,372
He died in nineteen eighty-eight,

254
00:12:05,372 --> 00:12:07,972
exactly two years after the disaster

255
00:12:11,172 --> 00:12:12,202
After Chernobyl,

256
00:12:12,202 --> 00:12:15,602
the remaining RBMK reactors were modified,

257
00:12:15,602 --> 00:12:18,402
and nuclear safety rules around the world

258
00:12:18,402 --> 00:12:19,842
became stricter

259
00:12:27,717 --> 00:12:28,747
Around the plant,

260
00:12:28,747 --> 00:12:31,547
an exclusion zone with a radius of thirty

261
00:12:31,547 --> 00:12:33,054
kilometers was set up

262
00:12:33,054 --> 00:12:33,907
Over time,

263
00:12:33,907 --> 00:12:36,787
about three hundred and fifty thousand people

264
00:12:36,787 --> 00:12:39,427
in Ukraine and Belarus had to leave their

265
00:12:39,427 --> 00:12:40,547
homes

266
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:44,610
Radiation spread across Europe,

267
00:12:44,610 --> 00:12:46,150
worst of all in Ukraine,

268
00:12:46,150 --> 00:12:47,910
Belarus and Russia

269
00:12:49,932 --> 00:12:52,762
Thousands of children drank milk contaminated

270
00:12:52,762 --> 00:12:54,642
with radioactive iodine,

271
00:12:54,642 --> 00:12:57,082
and later developed thyroid cancer

272
00:12:57,082 --> 00:12:58,042
Fortunately,

273
00:12:58,042 --> 00:13:00,122
most were successfully treated

274
00:13:02,473 --> 00:13:04,663
The death toll is still debated

275
00:13:04,663 --> 00:13:07,223
The initial official figure was thirty-one

276
00:13:07,223 --> 00:13:09,983
The World Health Organization estimates there

277
00:13:09,983 --> 00:13:12,583
could be about four thousand cancer deaths in

278
00:13:12,583 --> 00:13:13,543
the long run,

279
00:13:13,543 --> 00:13:15,863
while some other studies give much higher

280
00:13:15,863 --> 00:13:16,983
numbers

281
00:13:19,489 --> 00:13:21,359
In the basement of reactor four,

282
00:13:21,359 --> 00:13:24,559
a mass of hardened radioactive lava was found

283
00:13:24,559 --> 00:13:27,159
It is called the Elephant's Foot

284
00:13:29,556 --> 00:13:30,986
But here is the surprise:

285
00:13:30,986 --> 00:13:32,266
when people left,

286
00:13:32,266 --> 00:13:34,026
nature came back

287
00:13:36,594 --> 00:13:37,184
Wolves,

288
00:13:37,184 --> 00:13:37,704
moose,

289
00:13:37,704 --> 00:13:41,184
and even the rare wild Przewalski's horses now

290
00:13:41,184 --> 00:13:43,584
live in the exclusion zone

291
00:13:51,450 --> 00:13:52,800
In twenty sixteen,

292
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:54,240
a giant steel arch,

293
00:13:54,240 --> 00:13:56,640
weighing about thirty-six thousand tonnes,

294
00:13:56,640 --> 00:13:59,230
was slid into place to cover the old

295
00:13:59,230 --> 00:14:00,350
sarcophagus

296
00:14:02,859 --> 00:14:05,369
It was designed to last a hundred years,

297
00:14:05,369 --> 00:14:07,969
until the reactor can be safely taken apart

298
00:14:10,843 --> 00:14:12,153
In twenty twenty-two,

299
00:14:12,153 --> 00:14:13,513
when the war broke out,

300
00:14:13,513 --> 00:14:16,393
the plant was occupied by Russian troops for

301
00:14:16,393 --> 00:14:18,153
about five weeks

302
00:14:20,123 --> 00:14:21,553
On February fourteenth,

303
00:14:21,553 --> 00:14:22,953
twenty twenty-five,

304
00:14:22,953 --> 00:14:24,513
a drone struck the arch,

305
00:14:24,513 --> 00:14:26,873
punching a hole in it and starting a fire

306
00:14:26,873 --> 00:14:28,953
that smoldered for weeks

307
00:14:31,527 --> 00:14:33,637
No radiation leak was detected,

308
00:14:33,637 --> 00:14:36,757
but repairs are estimated to cost at least five

309
00:14:36,757 --> 00:14:38,517
hundred million euros

310
00:14:40,929 --> 00:14:42,399
In twenty twenty-six,

311
00:14:42,399 --> 00:14:44,319
forty years after the disaster,

312
00:14:44,319 --> 00:14:47,039
about two thousand people still work at the

313
00:14:47,039 --> 00:14:49,439
plant to keep it safe

314
00:14:51,838 --> 00:14:54,348
The area around reactor four will remain

315
00:14:54,348 --> 00:14:56,748
dangerous for thousands of years

316
00:15:01,283 --> 00:15:03,633
Chernobyl is a lesson about the cost of

317
00:15:03,633 --> 00:15:04,433
secrecy,

318
00:15:04,433 --> 00:15:07,033
and about the courage of ordinary people

319
00:15:08,427 --> 00:15:09,377
The firefighters,

320
00:15:09,377 --> 00:15:10,137
pilots,

321
00:15:10,137 --> 00:15:10,777
divers,

322
00:15:10,777 --> 00:15:13,657
miners and hundreds of thousands of liquidators

323
00:15:13,657 --> 00:15:16,057
stopped an even bigger disaster

324
00:15:17,509 --> 00:15:18,539
If you had the chance,

325
00:15:18,539 --> 00:15:21,059
would you dare to visit the Chernobyl exclusion

326
00:15:21,059 --> 00:15:21,539
zone?

327
00:15:21,539 --> 00:15:24,139
Let me know in the comments
